C++ conditionals look like Java's — `if` / `else if` / `else`:

```cpp
int score = 85;

if (score >= 90) {
    std::cout << "A grade" << std::endl;
} else if (score >= 80) {
    std::cout << "B grade" << std::endl;
} else {
    std::cout << "Keep practicing" << std::endl;
}
```

Operators: `==` (equal), `!=` (not equal), `>`, `<`, `>=`, `<=`.

Logical operators use words, like Java:

```cpp
int age = 20;
bool hasId = true;

if (age >= 18 && hasId) {
    std::cout << "Allowed in" << std::endl;
}

if (age < 13 || age > 60) {
    std::cout << "Special discount" << std::endl;
}

if (!hasId) {
    std::cout << "Need an ID" << std::endl;
}
```

The **switch** statement is a clean alternative when comparing one value against many constants:

```cpp
int day = 2;
switch (day) {
    case 1: std::cout << "Mon"; break;
    case 2: std::cout << "Tue"; break;
    default: std::cout << "Other";
}
```

Don't forget `break;` in each case — without it, execution *falls through* to the next case.

